2026-11-13T03:14:44.6543981Z # LexMachina
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6544299Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6544615Z LexMachina is a **query-specific visual legal-research system for Swiss law**.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6545138Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6546837Z A user asks a legal question. The product retrieves relevant case law, generates a sourced answer, independently searches for contrary/limiting/exception authorities, and displays the retrieved jurisprudence as an interactive map so minority lines and outliers are harder to miss.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6548655Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6548803Z Current MVP flow:
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6548965Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6549046Z ```
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6549226Z question
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6549470Z   -> live OpenCaseLaw retrieval + deterministic dedupe
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6549835Z   -> query-conditioned clusters/map (rendered immediately)
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6550167Z   -> lazy evidence hydration
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6550429Z   -> source-constrained generative answer
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6550754Z   -> independent adversarial counter-authority pass
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6551115Z   -> patch answer/evidence/statuses without moving the map
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6551457Z   -> clickable decision/source inspection
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6551749Z ```
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6551841Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6552458Z The repository is operated by an autonomous OpenCode/Ox engineering factory. The factory optimizes for **minimal repository-owner attention** and aggressive reuse of the prior LexMachina research/codebase.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6553134Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6553734Z `main` is intentionally a lightweight control plane. Full historical artifacts remain on accepted `lab/*` and cycle branches; only compact accepted-state summaries are mirrored to `main`.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6554542Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6554547Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6554655Z ## Run the v45 query-map product
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6554814Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6555211Z The v45 MVP entry point is `product/query_app.py`; `product/server.py` is the preserved legacy 174k-map server, not the query-product hot path.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6555672Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6555944Z The normal path uses live OpenCaseLaw retrieval and the free/local generative provider abstraction:
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6556439Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6556517Z ```bash
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6556741Z pip install -r product/requirements-query.txt
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6557051Z python product/query_app.py --port 8787
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6557307Z ```
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6557406Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6557985Z Open `http://127.0.0.1:8787/`. The UI renders the retrieved map first, then lazily hydrates evidence and enriches that same map with the sourced answer and independent counter-authority audit.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6558590Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6559584Z A local OpenAI-compatible backend (Ollama / LM Studio / vLLM) can be used by setting `LEX_GENAUDIT_BACKEND=local`, `LEX_OPENAI_BASE_URL`, and optionally `LEX_OPENAI_MODEL`. If no generative provider succeeds, LEX keeps the map and sources and visibly falls back to the extractive answer instead of pretending the generative audit succeeded.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6560632Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6560745Z For deterministic offline development/tests:
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6560942Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6561020Z ```bash
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6561247Z python product/query_app.py --fixture --port 8787
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6561575Z pytest -q product/tests/test_query_map_mvp.py
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6561849Z ```
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6561941Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6562294Z The extractive TF-IDF answer is a degraded fallback only. Normal v45 behavior is the promoted `generative-audit` contract 2.2.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6562810Z === query_app head ===
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6563111Z """One-command local server for the LexMachina query-map product.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6563355Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6563470Z     python product/query_app.py --port 8787
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6563865Z     python product/query_app.py --port 8787 --fixture     # offline, deterministic
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6564418Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6564697Z The UI runs a three-stage job pipeline so a human never waits on a blocking
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6565055Z HTTP request:
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6565182Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6565366Z     GET /api/query/start?q=&n=      -> first-page map in seconds (run_id)
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6565820Z     GET /api/query/expand?run_id=   -> starts/queries the background expansion
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6566377Z     GET /api/query/enrich?run_id=   -> starts the answer + counter-authority job
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6566840Z     GET /api/query/status?run_id=   -> progress for both jobs (polled by the UI)
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6567291Z     GET /api/query/bundle?run_id=   -> current bundle (resume after a reload)
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6567557Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6567729Z Why jobs instead of one blocking call (measured live on 2026-10-15, run
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6568179Z 38665681083): ``/api/query/start`` took **136.79 s** before the first point was
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6568644Z drawn (136.61 s of it upstream OpenCaseLaw) and ``/api/query/enrich`` a further
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6569106Z **150.70 s**.  A browser fetch that long is indistinguishable from a broken
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6569559Z product, and a reload loses the run.  Each stage now runs in a worker thread,
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6570025Z reports progress through ``/api/query/status`` and is deduplicated, so a
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6570436Z double-click cannot start two three-minute model runs.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6570658Z 
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6570853Z Every stage is still reachable as one blocking call with ``&wait_s=<seconds>``
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6571319Z (scripts, CI, the reality-check legs), so nothing that worked before is lost.
2026-11-13T03:14:44.6571665Z """
